{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.046,
            0.364,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.087,
            0.847,
            0.143
          ],
          "content": "Now, the next step is multiplying this result by 5. This gives the product of $\\frac { 5 } { 4 }$ and $\\frac { 3 } { 2 } ;$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.148,
            0.349,
            0.185
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{5}{4} \\times \\frac{3}{2} = 5 \\times \\frac{3}{8} = \\frac{15}{8}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.205,
            0.846,
            0.249
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Connection between the Area of a Rectangle and Fraction Multiplication"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.258,
            0.847,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "In the Fig. 8.3, what is the length and breadth of the shaded rectangle? Since we started with a unit square (of side 1 unit), the length and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.297,
            0.449,
            0.332
          ],
          "content": "breadth are $\\frac { 1 } { 2 }$ unit and $\\frac { 1 } { 4 }$ unit."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.339,
            0.599,
            0.432
          ],
          "content": "What is the area of this rectangle? We see that 8 such rectangles give the square of area 1 square unit. So, the area of each rectangle is $\\frac { 1 } { 8 }$ square units."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.609,
            0.307,
            0.833,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.433,
            0.599,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": "Do you see any relation between the area and the product of length and breadth?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.713,
            0.444,
            0.778,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 8.3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.483,
            0.846,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": "The area of a rectangle of fractional sides equals the product of its sides."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.52,
            0.847,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "In general, if we want to find the product of two fractions, we can find the area of the rectangle formed with the two fractions as its sides."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.565,
            0.293,
            0.589
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.592,
            0.846,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "1. Find the following products. Use a unit square as a whole for representing the fractions:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.637,
            0.31,
            0.793
          ],
          "content": "(a) $\\frac { 1 } { 3 } \\times \\frac { 1 } { 5 }$ (b) $\\frac { 1 } { 4 } \\times \\frac { 1 } { 3 }$ (c) $\\frac{1}{5} \\times \\frac{1}{2}$ (d) $\\frac{1}{6} \\times \\frac{1}{5}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.796,
            0.369,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "Now, find $\\frac { 1 } { 1 2 } \\times \\frac { 1 } { 1 8 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.938,
            0.192,
            0.955
          ],
          "content": "180"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
